476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 387 0 R
/E (Policy improvement is the process of updating the policy to be greedy with respect to the current value function. After evaluating a policy to obtain its value function V^\\pi\(s\), we can improve the policy by making it greedy with respect to V^\\pi. This means choosing actions that lead to the states with the highest expected value, effectively pushing the agent towards more rewarding paths.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
477 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 387 0 R
/Pg 15 0 R
/A [1954 0 R 0]
/Alt (preencoded.png)
/K [2]
>>
endobj
478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 387 0 R
/E (This equation defines a new, improved policy \\pi' by selecting the action a that maximizes the expected return from state s, considering the immediate reward r and the discounted value of the next state s' under the current value function V^\\pi.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
479 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 387 0 R
/E (Policy Improvement Theorem: For any two policies \\pi and \\pi', if \\pi' is an improvement over \\pi such that V^{\\pi'}\(s\) \\ge V^\\pi\(s\) for all s \\in S, then \\pi' is better than or equal to \\pi for all states, and \\pi' is strictly better if there is any state s where V^{\\pi'}\(s\) > V^\\pi\(s\).)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
