/Pg 21 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (TD\(0\) combines ideas from Monte Carlo and Dynamic Programming. Like Monte Carlo methods, TD\(0\) learns directly from experience without a model of the environment. Like Dynamic Programming, it updates its estimates based in part on other learned estimates, a process known as bootstrapping.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 393 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/A [1987 0 R 0]
/Alt (preencoded.png)
/K [2]
>>
endobj
579 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (In this update equation, r + \\gamma V\(s'\) is the TD target, which is an estimate of the expected return. This target uses the immediate reward r and the estimated value of the next state V\(s'\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (The TD error is defined as \\delta_t = r + \\gamma V\(s'\) - V\(s\). This error represents the difference between the estimated value of the current state and the TD target, which is a one-step lookahead estimate of the actual return.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
581 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 393 0 R
/E (Unlike Monte Carlo methods, which wait until the end of an episode to compute the return G_t and update V\(s\), TD\(0\) bootstraps by updating its estimate for V\(s\) using the estimated value V\(s'\) of the next state, as soon as it transitions to s'. This allows for updates after each time step rather than only at episode termination.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
582 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 394 0 R
/T (SARSA: On-Policy TD Control)
/E (SARSA: On-Policy TD Control)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
583 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E (SARSA \(State-Action-Reward-State-Action\) is an on-policy temporal-difference \(TD\) control algorithm. It learns the optimal policy by estimating the action-value function Q\(s,a\) for the policy currently being followed. Unlike Q-learning, which is off-policy, SARSA updates its Q-values based on the actual next action a' taken by the agent, according to its current policy, rather than the greedy action.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
584 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 394 0 R
/Pg 22 0 R
/A [1988 0 R 0]
/Alt (preencoded.png)
/K [2]
>>
endobj
585 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E (Here, Q\(s,a\) is the estimated value of taking action a in state s. The agent receives a reward r and transitions to state s', where it then selects the next action a' using its current policy. The update uses the value of the actual state-action pair \(s',a'\).)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
586 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 394 0 R
/E (On-policy learning means that the algorithm learns about the policy that it is currently executing \(the "behavior policy"\). This implies that the agent's exploration strategy is inherent in the policy being evaluated and improved. SARSA is considered an on-policy method because its update rule directly depends on the action a' selected by the same policy that is being learned.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
