611 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 395 0 R
/E (Q-Learning is an off-policy temporal-difference \(TD\) control algorithm that aims to find the optimal action-value function Q^*\(s,a\). It learns the optimal policy by directly approximating the optimal Q-function, irrespective of the policy currently being followed by the agent to generate experience.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
612 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 395 0 R
/Pg 23 0 R
/A [1996 0 R 0]
/Alt (preencoded.png)
/K [2]
>>
endobj
613 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 395 0 R
/E (In this update equation, Q\(s,a\) is the estimated value of taking action a in state s. The agent receives a reward r and transitions to state s'. The key difference from SARSA is the use of \\max_{a'} Q\(s',a'\), which represents the maximum possible Q-value for the next state s' across all possible actions a'. This means Q-Learning assumes that from the next state, the optimal action will be chosen, regardless of what action was actually taken by the agent's behavior policy to arrive at s'.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
614 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 395 0 R
/E (Off-policy learning means that the algorithm learns about the optimal policy \(the target policy\) while following a different, often exploratory, policy \(the behavior policy\). Q-Learning uses a behavior policy \(e.g., an \\epsilon-greedy policy\) to explore the environment and collect experience, but its update rule directly estimates the optimal Q-values, effectively learning the greedy policy with respect to Q regardless of the exploration strategy. This allows for greater flexibility in balancing exploration and exploitation.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
615 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 395 0 R
/E (A major advantage of Q-Learning is its ability to learn an optimal policy even when the agent is following a sub-optimal or exploratory policy. This separation of behavior and target policies can make it more efficient in finding optimal solutions. However, a disadvantage can be its sensitivity to exploration strategies; poor exploration might lead to sub-optimal policies if not all relevant state-action pairs are sufficiently visited.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
