<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 401 0 R
/E (During training, instead of learning directly from the most recent interaction, mini-batches of these stored transitions are randomly sampled from the replay buffer. This random sampling is key to breaking the temporal correlations inherent in sequential experience, which would otherwise destabilize the neural network's learning process.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
761 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 401 0 R
/E (Key benefits of using Experience Replay include:)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
762 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2038 0 R
/E (It breaks the correlation between consecutive samples, making the training data more independently and identically distributed \(i.i.d.\), which is beneficial for deep learning.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
763 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2039 0 R
/E (It improves sample efficiency by allowing the agent to reuse past experiences multiple times for learning, rather than discarding them after a single use.)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
764 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2040 0 R
/E (It enables off-policy learning by allowing the agent to learn about the optimal policy \(target policy\) from data generated by a different behavior policy \(e.g., an \\epsilon-greedy policy\).)
/Pg 29 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
