784 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 402 0 R
/E (Target Networks are a key innovation in Deep Q-Networks \(DQN\) designed to stabilize the learning process. In DQN, we use two separate neural networks: the online Q-network \(with weights \\theta\) and the target Q-network \(with weights \\theta^-\).)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
785 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 402 0 R
/E (The online Q-network is responsible for predicting the Q-values for the current state-action pairs, Q\(s,a; \\theta\), and its weights are updated at every training step. In contrast, the target Q-network is a copy of the online Q-network, but its weights \\theta^- are kept fixed for a certain number of training steps and only updated periodically.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
786 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 402 0 R
/E (The target Q-network is used to compute the target Q-values, which are crucial for the Bellman equation. Specifically, the target Q-value is calculated using the target network's prediction for the next state, \\max_{a'} Q\(s',a'; \\theta^-\). This approach helps to decouple the target from the online network's rapidly changing weights.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
787 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 402 0 R
/Pg 30 0 R
/A [2045 0 R 0]
/Alt (preencoded.png)
/K [4]
>>
endobj
788 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 402 0 R
/E (The primary reason for using target networks is to prevent the problem of a "moving target." Without a separate target network, the Q-values used to define the learning target would constantly change with every update of the online network. This non-stationarity can lead to oscillations, divergence, and unstable learning. By freezing the target network's weights for several steps, we create a more stable target, allowing the online network to converge more smoothly.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
789 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 402 0 R
/E (The interaction between the two networks and the update process can be visualized as follows:)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
