/E (The diagram above illustrates the iterative process of policy gradient methods. The agent, governed by its policy, interacts with the environment, taking actions and observing rewards. These rewards serve as feedback, indicating the quality of the taken actions. Policy gradients then use this feedback to directly adjust the policy parameters \\theta, making actions that led to favorable outcomes more probable in the future, and less favorable actions less probable. This direct adjustment allows the policy to continuously improve its decision-making capabilities over time.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [26]
>>
