952 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (REINFORCE, also known as Monte Carlo Policy Gradient, is one of the simplest and most foundational policy gradient algorithms in reinforcement learning. It directly optimizes a parameterized policy \\pi\(a|s; \\theta\) using Monte Carlo estimates of the return. Unlike value-based methods, REINFORCE focuses on adjusting the policy parameters to increase the probability of actions that lead to higher cumulative rewards over an entire episode.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
953 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (The core idea of REINFORCE is to update the policy parameters \\theta in a direction that makes good actions \(actions that lead to high future returns\) more probable. The update rule is given by:)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
954 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 409 0 R
/Pg 37 0 R
/A [2097 0 R 0]
/Alt (preencoded.png)
/K [3]
>>
endobj
955 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (Here, \\alpha is the learning rate, \\nabla\\log \\pi\(a_t|s_t; \\theta\) is the gradient of the log-probability of taking action a_t in state s_t at time t, and G_t is the cumulative discounted return from time t until the end of the episode. This means that the algorithm scales the policy gradient by the total reward received after taking that action, reinforcing actions that led to high returns.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
956 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (A key characteristic of REINFORCE is its reliance on complete episode returns \(G_t\). The algorithm must wait until an entire episode is completed to calculate the full return G_t before performing a policy update. This episodic nature means that REINFORCE is typically applied to tasks with finite horizons or where episodes naturally terminate.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
