1060 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 415 0 R
/E (Actor-critic methods represent a powerful class of reinforcement learning algorithms that combine the strengths of both policy gradient \(actor\) and value-based \(critic\) approaches. This hybrid architecture allows for more stable and efficient learning, particularly in complex environments with continuous action spaces.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1061 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 415 0 R
/E (The core of the actor-critic architecture consists of two main components:)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1062 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2142 0 R
/E (Actor Network \(\\pi\(a|s; \\theta\)\): This component is a neural network parameterized by \\theta that takes the current state \(s\) as input and outputs a probability distribution over actions \(a\) or directly outputs the actions themselves. Its role is to learn an optimal policy, determining which actions to take in a given state.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1063 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2143 0 R
/E (Critic Network \(V\(s; \\phi\)\): This component is another neural network, parameterized by \\phi, that estimates the value of the current state \(s\). Specifically, it outputs the expected return \(e.g., expected discounted sum of future rewards\) from that state under the current policy. Its role is to evaluate the actions taken by the actor.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1064 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 415 0 R
/E (The interaction between the actor and critic is central to the learning process. The actor network selects an action based on its current policy. The critic network then evaluates the quality of this action by comparing the observed reward and the estimated value of the next state to its own prediction. This evaluation signal \(often in the form of an advantage estimate\) is then used to update both the actor's policy and the critic's value function.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1065 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 415 0 R
/E (A key concept in actor-critic methods is the advantage function, defined as A\(s,a\) = Q\(s,a\) - V\(s\). Here, Q\(s,a\) represents the state-action value function \(the expected return from taking action a in state s\), and V\(s\) is the state-value function estimated by the critic. The advantage function indicates how much better or worse a particular action is compared to the average expected return from that state. This allows the actor to update its policy by favoring actions that yield higher-than-expected rewards without solely relying on the absolute reward signal, which can have high variance.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
