>>
endobj
1178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (The line graphs above visually represent the learning progress of different algorithms over training steps. Typically, A3C and DQN demonstrate rapid learning and achieve high average rewards relatively quickly, though DQN might exhibit more variance. A2C offers a more stable, albeit sometimes slower, learning trajectory, while REINFORCE often shows a slower convergence with higher variability in its performance, emphasizing the challenge of high variance in pure policy gradient methods.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [34]
>>
endobj
1179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 418 0 R
/T (Trade-offs in Actor-Critic Methods)
/E (Trade-offs in Actor-Critic Methods)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [35]
>>
endobj
1180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (The selection of an actor-critic method often involves navigating critical trade-offs between sample efficiency, computational cost, and stability. Algorithms like A3C, while highly sample efficient and stable due to their asynchronous nature and decorrelated experiences, can introduce complexity in implementation and debugging due to managing multiple parallel agents. A2C simplifies this by using synchronous updates, which reduces computational overhead and complexity but sacrifices some sample efficiency. Deep Q-Networks \(DQN\), though not purely actor-critic, are noted for their strong performance but can sometimes suffer from instability and hyperparameter sensitivity. REINFORCE, a foundational policy gradient method, is simple but typically exhibits high variance and low sample efficiency, necessitating many samples to learn effectively. Understanding these trade-offs is crucial for choosing the most appropriate algorithm for a given reinforcement learning problem and environment constraints.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [36]
>>
endobj
