1224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 420 0 R
/E (The journey through reinforcement learning reveals a spectrum of powerful approaches, each uniquely suited to different challenges. We've explored five major paradigms:)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2196 0 R
/E (Dynamic Programming \(DP\): Model-based, guaranteeing optimal policies when the environment model is fully known.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2197 0 R
/E (Monte Carlo \(MC\) & Temporal Difference \(TD\): Model-free methods that learn directly from experience. MC for episodic tasks, TD for continuous learning with bootstrapping.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2198 0 R
/E (Deep Q-Networks \(DQN\): A breakthrough value-based approach combining Q-learning with deep neural networks to tackle high-dimensional observation spaces with discrete actions.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2199 0 R
/E (Policy Gradient \(PG\): Directly optimizes the policy, making it effective for continuous control problems and when a value function is hard to learn.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2200 0 R
/E (Actor-Critic \(AC\): A hybrid method that combines the strengths of policy gradient \(actor\) and value-based methods \(critic\) for a more balanced and often more stable learning process.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
